CLAIMS THAT NEED NO VERSION
   ud.name('A')           LATIN CAPITAL LETTER A       assigned in 1991 and never touched
   ud.category('A')       Lu                           a letter, uppercase -- settled
   'ß'.upper()            SS                           a case mapping fixed long ago
   len('😀'.encode())      4                            UTF-8's rules, not the table's

   Assignment is a one-way door: once a code point has a name and a
   category, the consortium does not reassign it. So a claim about a
   character that already existed when your Python was built is a claim
   about Unicode, and it will read the same in five years.

CLAIMS THAT DO NOT SURVIVE WITHOUT ONE
   unicodedata.unidata_version            the whole question -- and it is a property of the LIBRARY, not the machine
   how many code points are assigned      grows every release; ~5,000 were added in one recent year
   ud.name(chr(0x1FA00 + n))              raises for a character not yet in YOUR table, and returns a name in a newer one
   whether a string 'is emoji'            the emoji list is versioned separately from Unicode itself
   str.isprintable() on a recent char     depends on a category your table may not have yet

THE PART THAT SURPRISES PEOPLE
   There is no 'the' Unicode version on a computer. Every language ships
   its OWN copy of the table: Python's is in unicodedata, Rust's is
   compiled into core, your database has another, and your browser a
   fourth. They are updated on different schedules by different people,
   so two programs on one machine can disagree about whether a character
   exists -- and neither is out of date, exactly. They were built at
   different times.

   That is why the version belongs in the bug report next to the output,
   and why 'it works on my machine' has one more meaning here than it
   does anywhere else.

HOW TO ASK EACH ONE, WHEN YOU NEED THE STAMP
   python3 -c 'import unicodedata; print(unicodedata.unidata_version)'
   rustc         -- see char::UNICODE_VERSION in core
   psql          -- SELECT icu_unicode_version();
   node          -- process.versions.unicode
